# gmktec-xubuntu.env — the per-node DATA the bare-metal rebuild procedure
# consumes for the fleet's SECOND pool node.
#
# THIS FILE CARRIES NO PROCEDURE, and it is not a copy of one. It is read by the
# SAME ../storage-layout.sh, the same ../../provision-k3s.sh and the same
# ../../phase2/install-node.sh the first node's profile is read by, through the
# same shared parser ../profile.sh. That is the whole claim this file exists to
# make good on: a second pool node is a second PROFILE, never a second
# procedure and never a hand-edited copy of one
# (SPECIFICATION/non-functional-requirements.md §"Runner-pool node rebuild
# recipe", "One profile per node").
#
# THIS NODE IS THE PLAN'S REHEARSAL OF THE RECIPE. The first node's profile was
# written from a rebuild that had already been done BY HAND, so the procedure
# describes it rather than having produced it. This node has not been built yet,
# so building it is the first end-to-end exercise of the one procedure — which
# is what the specification section's standing rehearsal obligation asks for.
# Read ../README.md §"Rehearsed before trusted" before the first live run.
#
# FORMAT: identical to the first node's, because one parser reads them both —
# `KEY=value` one per line, `#` comments and blank lines ignored, PARSED and
# never sourced, list-valued keys holding space-separated `:`-delimited records.
# Read ./poweredge-xubuntu.env's header for the format in full; it is not
# restated here, because a second copy of it is a second thing to drift.
#
# HOW THIS NODE DIFFERS FROM THE FIRST, since that is what the file is for:
#   * it has NO storage controller, so the virtual-disk stage has nothing to do;
#   * it KEEPS THE OPERATING SYSTEM IT ALREADY RUNS. Its single NVMe drive
#     already carries the root filesystem and the EFI system partition it boots
#     from, so the plan is `free-space`: only the drive's unpartitioned tail is
#     the procedure's to take, and the two partitions that are not are named in
#     PRESERVED_PARTITIONS, which no `--i-consent-to-destroy` unlocks;
#   * it joins the first node's cluster as an `agent` rather than forming one;
#   * it admits NO churn slots yet (ADMISSION_CAPACITY_C=0): the capacity this
#     node contributes is derived and set by a later item of the same plan, and
#     0 with a NoSchedule taint is what "joined, and taking nothing" looks like
#     as data.
#
# PROVENANCE of the values below, so a rebuild knows what is measured and what
# is not.
#   MEASURED ON THE HOST 2026-09-06, and therefore trustworthy as written:
#     - the drive: a Lexar NM790 2 TB at `nvme0n1`, whose `p1` is a 466 GiB ext4
#       filesystem mounted at `/`, whose `p2` is the 1 GiB EFI system partition,
#       and whose remaining 1441 GiB is UNPARTITIONED. That tail is what the
#       three tiers below are sized against;
#     - that the host has NO RAID controller of any kind;
#     - the wired interface `eno1` at 192.168.1.156/24;
#     - the first node's Kubernetes API at https://192.168.1.200:6443, which is
#       the address CLUSTER_JOIN_ADDRESS names.
#   NOT MEASURED, and marked `# UNVERIFIED` on its own line below: every value
#   whose only consumer is the base-OS stage (../base-os-install.sh) — the
#   labels of the two partitions this node already boots from, the firmware boot
#   entry's label, and the operator account's name. See "The base-OS stage is
#   not a stage this node runs" below for why those are inert here rather than
#   merely unconfirmed.
#
# ONE THING THE HOST RUNS THAT NOTHING HERE MAY TOUCH: a
# `local-homelab-llama-server.service` inside a 64 GiB firmware iGPU carveout.
# It is not the CI pool's, no key below describes it, and no stage this profile
# drives goes near it. It is recorded because the carveout is why this node's
# usable tail is 1441 GiB and not the whole drive, and because a future reader
# sizing the tiers up will otherwise find the missing 64 GiB and "fix" it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_NAME=gmktec-xubuntu

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage controller and its virtual disk — this node has none.
#
# CONTROLLER_KIND=none is what makes every CONTROLLER_* and VD_* value below
# permissibly EMPTY, and it is what makes stage 1 skip the virtual-disk stage
# outright rather than partition a device some controller was expected to
# present. Measured: the host carries no RAID controller, so there is no CLI to
# name and no virtual disk to build. The bare NVMe drive IS the storage.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONTROLLER_KIND=none
CONTROLLER_CLI=
CONTROLLER_ID=
VD_ENCLOSURE=
VD_SLOTS=
VD_RAID_LEVEL=
VD_STRIP_KIB=
VD_CACHE_POLICY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Partition table on the device the operating system already boots from
#
# DISK_PLAN=free-space: this node's `/dev/nvme0n1` carries its running Xubuntu.
# The plan therefore adds exactly ONE partition, in the largest free region,
# and touches nothing else on the device — no `wipefs`, no `sgdisk --zap-all`,
# and no `mkfs` over the EFI system partition the node boots through.
#
# PRESERVED_PARTITIONS names the two partitions that are not the procedure's.
# It is a STANDING REFUSAL and not a consent question: ../storage-layout.sh
# scans the arguments of every mutating command it is about to issue and exits
# non-zero if any word is one of these, and `--i-consent-to-destroy` naming one
# does not unlock it (../README.md §"The two disk plans").
#
# The partition NUMBER the new partition takes is NOT stated here: stage 1
# reads the next free number off the device's own table, because the existing
# partitions belong to an operating system this procedure did not install and
# their count is not the procedure's to know. On the measured table (1 and 2
# taken) that resolves to 3, which is the number VOLUME_GROUPS below spells out
# as a path. DRY-RUN FIRST AND READ THE NUMBER IT DERIVED: those two must
# agree, and only the dry run can tell you they do.
#
# ESP_DEVICE points at `p2`, not `p1` — the measured layout puts the 1 GiB EFI
# system partition SECOND, after the 466 GiB root. Under `free-space` the ESP is
# described so the later stages can find it and is never made.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DISK_PLAN=free-space
PRESERVED_PARTITIONS=/dev/nvme0n1p1 /dev/nvme0n1p2
TARGET_DEVICE=/dev/nvme0n1
ESP_DEVICE=/dev/nvme0n1p2
ESP_SIZE=1GiB
ESP_FSTYPE=vfat
# UNVERIFIED: the label the existing EFI system partition already carries. Read
# only by the base-OS stage, which this node does not run — see below.
ESP_LABEL=ESP
PV_PARTLABEL=lvm

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Volume groups — one per medium. Records are `<vg>:<physical-volume-device>`.
#
# ONE group, because there is one medium: the tail partition of the single NVMe
# drive. `nvmea` is the fleet's name for "the first NVMe group on this node",
# the same name the first node's first NVMe group carries; the two are different
# hosts' groups and never meet.
#
# WHY A KERNEL PATH AND NOT `/dev/disk/by-id/`. The first node's profile
# addresses its NVMe physical volumes by serial ONLY, because behind a PCIe
# switch the `nvme0n1`/`nvme1n1` ordering can shift across boots and that node
# has to tell two identical drives apart. This node has ONE NVMe drive, and the
# partition named here is one the procedure creates on the very device the node
# already boots from — TARGET_DEVICE above names it by kernel path for exactly
# that reason. A by-id path here would have to be the same device spelled two
# ways in one file, with no second drive for either spelling to disambiguate.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VOLUME_GROUPS=nvmea:/dev/nvme0n1p3

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logical volumes — records are `<vg>:<lv>:<size>:<fstype>:<label>`.
#
# All three tiers on the one group, sized to the measured 1441 GiB tail:
# 350 + 525 + 525 = 1400 GiB, leaving ~41 GiB unallocated for LVM metadata and
# for the headroom a tier extension needs.
#
# NO `root` and NO `swap` record, and their absence is the plan: this node's
# root is the ext4 filesystem on the preserved `p1`, not a logical volume, and
# it has no swap volume. The role LVs carry the same NAMES they do on the first
# node, which is what makes a tier's identity its LABEL rather than its device
# (../../phase2/README.md §"Why labels, and the 16-byte rule").
#
# The filesystem types MUST agree with ../../phase2/storage-layout/migrate-tier.sh's
# `role_fstype` and with install-storage-layout.sh's fstab lines: `ci-cache` and
# `ci-containerd` ext4, `ci-workvols` XFS with reflink (stage 1 passes
# `-m reflink=1`), which is what lets the warm-cache seed give every job its own
# inodes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
LOGICAL_VOLUMES=nvmea:ci-cache:350GiB:ext4:ci-cache nvmea:ci-containerd:525GiB:ext4:ci-containerd nvmea:ci-workvols:525GiB:xfs:ci-workvols

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Role-labelled storage tiers — records are `<role-label>:<vg>`.
#
# All three on `nvmea`, because there is one medium to put them on. The first
# node spreads its three across three groups; that is a fact about that node's
# hardware and not a rule of the procedure, which is why the placement is data.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ROLE_TIERS=ci-cache:nvmea ci-containerd:nvmea ci-workvols:nvmea

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Base operating system — and THE BASE-OS STAGE IS NOT A STAGE THIS NODE RUNS.
#
# ../base-os-install.sh installs an operating system onto the root LOGICAL
# VOLUME its profile names. This node's root is the ext4 filesystem on the
# preserved `/dev/nvme0n1p1`; keeping it is the entire reason DISK_PLAN is
# `free-space`. Running that stage here would be asking to debootstrap over the
# operating system the plan exists to preserve, and it REFUSES rather than
# doing so: ROOT_LABEL below names no LOGICAL_VOLUMES record, which is a
# fail-closed exit before the stage mounts anything. The refusal is the correct
# outcome; its message describes the mechanism (no record carries the label)
# rather than the reason (this node keeps its OS), which is a wording defect in
# that stage and not a reason to give this file a root volume it does not have.
#
# Every key in this section is therefore INERT for this node. They are stated
# because the parser requires them present and non-empty, and they are stated
# EQUAL TO THE FIRST NODE'S so that the one value that is a fleet fact — the
# release the pool's nodes run — is the same string in both files rather than
# two strings that can drift. SWAP_LABEL is the one exception and is EMPTY: the
# key's own rule permits empty for a node with no swap volume, and this node has
# none, so naming one would be the only false statement in the section.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
OS_DISTRIBUTION=ubuntu
OS_RELEASE=resolute
OS_ARCHITECTURE=amd64
OS_MIRROR=http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu
OS_SECURITY_MIRROR=http://security.ubuntu.com/ubuntu
OS_COMPONENTS=main restricted universe multiverse
KERNEL_PACKAGE=linux-image-generic
INITRAMFS_GENERATOR=dracut
# UNVERIFIED: the label the existing ext4 root on /dev/nvme0n1p1 carries. Inert
# for this node, per the section header above.
ROOT_LABEL=root
SWAP_LABEL=
# UNVERIFIED: the label of this node's existing firmware boot entry. Inert.
BOOT_ENTRY_LABEL=ubuntu
# UNVERIFIED: the name of the sudo-capable admin ../../provision-k3s.sh and
# ../../phase2/install-node.sh are run as on this node. Inert for the base-OS
# stage; the account itself already exists, since the node is already running.
OPERATOR_ACCOUNT=ci-admin
OPERATOR_GROUPS=sudo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network — measured.
#
# This node PINS both, where the first node pins neither. The pin is not a
# preference: a node whose wired and wireless interfaces can share a subnet can
# otherwise register with the cluster at an address the cluster cannot reach it
# on, and ../../provision-k3s.sh derives `--node-ip` from NODE_ADDRESS. The
# address is stated as the netplan CIDR the base-OS stage would write; the k3s
# stage takes the bare address out of it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_NETWORK_INTERFACE=eno1
NODE_ADDRESS=192.168.1.156/24

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cluster — consumed by ../../provision-k3s.sh and ../../phase2/install-node.sh.
#
# CLUSTER_ROLE=agent selects BOTH the k3s install command (`agent --server …`
# rather than `server …`) and the step plan install-node.sh runs (the node-local
# subset, skipping the cluster-side and datastore steps).
#
# CLUSTER_TOKEN_FILE is the PATH on the node holding the token k3s reads AT RUN
# TIME to authenticate to CLUSTER_JOIN_ADDRESS — never the token, because this
# tree carries no secret. Place it there (mode 0600, root:root) before
# provisioning; ../../provision-k3s.sh REFUSES, in `--dry-run` too, if the file
# is absent or readable beyond its owner.
#
# NODE_TAINTS carries `node-role/ci=pending:NoSchedule`, and it is doing real
# work: this node joins the cluster CLOSED. Until the plan's capacity item
# derives what this node can actually admit, no workflow pod may land here, and
# a NoSchedule taint plus ADMISSION_CAPACITY_C=0 is that state expressed twice,
# on purpose — the taint stops the scheduler, the capacity stops Kueue's
# admission. Removing either one alone does not open the node, and neither is
# this file's to remove until that item says so.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CLUSTER_ROLE=agent
CLUSTER_JOIN_ADDRESS=https://192.168.1.200:6443
CLUSTER_TOKEN_FILE=/etc/rancher/k3s/agent-join-token
NODE_TAINTS=node-role/ci=pending:NoSchedule
ADMISSION_CAPACITY_C=0
